  "skillId": "primitive-power-functions",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "The **primitive** of $x^{n}$ is found by adding $1$ to the power, then dividing by the new power. This works for every real $n$ except $n=-1$. Always add the constant of integration $+C$.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\displaystyle\\int x^{n}\\,dx=\\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C$ for every real $n\\ne-1$.",
      "A constant multiplier stays out the front: $\\displaystyle\\int kx^{n}\\,dx=\\dfrac{kx^{n+1}}{n+1}+C$.",
      "Integrate a sum term by term, and write a single $+C$ at the end.",
      "A constant integrates to $kx$, since $k=kx^{0}$.",
      "The rule fails at $n=-1$, because $\\dfrac{x^{0}}{0}$ divides by zero.",
      "Rewrite an algebraic denominator as a negative index first, such as $\\dfrac{1}{x^{3}}=x^{-3}$.",
      "Rewrite a root as a fractional index first, such as $\\sqrt{x}=x^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{x}=x^{\\frac{1}{3}}$.",
      "Expand a product and split a fraction before integrating; there is no reverse product or quotient rule.",
      "Check any primitive by differentiating it, which should return the original function."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rewrite roots, algebraic denominators and products in the form $kx^{n}$",
      "Add $1$ to each power",
      "Divide each term by its new power",
      "Add the constant of integration $+C$"
    ]
  },
